<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tacitus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 56–c. 120</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tacitus was a Roman historian and senator of the late first and early second centuries CE, author of the Annals, the Histories and other works. His writings are a major source for the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and its frontiers. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garamantian-empire">Garamantian Empire</a> note that Tacitus, writing around 109 CE, still described the Garamantes of the Libyan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> as unconquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacitus.jpg" alt="Tacitus" width="318" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pe-Jo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-_Parlament-Tacitus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
